{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>I have declared this day to YHWH your Elohim.</b> It would have been fitting for him to say 'to YHWH our Elohim,' and not remove himself from the collective of faith in divinity. But had he said so, his faith would not have been clarified for us, nor would we know his intent — to whom he calls YHWH, and who it is whose divinity he accepts upon himself. But now, when he comes before the priest (the high priest), who is specially designated for the service of the Elohim of Israel and knows Him — when he says 'to YHWH your Elohim,' the doubt is removed from us and his good faith is clarified, for he testifies about himself that the Elohim of the priest is his Elohim, for whose sake he came to the Land." "<b>And it is possible</b> that the expression 'I have declared' belongs to the concept of drawing down, in the manner of (Psalms 51:17–18): ״ופי יגיד תהלתך״ — 'and my mouth declares Your praise' — for one who prays draws down the power. And it is written (Deuteronomy 10:21): ״הוא תהלתך והוא אלהיך״ — 'He is your praise and He is your Elohim.' And the one who comes to the Holy Land draws down the power that corresponds to her; all the more so the priest, through the offerings that he offers there. And therefore he mentioned there 'to YHWH your Elohim,' according to the practice of Mishneh Torah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:07.831935142Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}